{% set local_problem %}
<p>Here's what 85% humidity does to the wood around your roofline:</p>

<p>Fascia boards absorb moisture from the air constantly. In Scottsboro's lake-effect climate, they never fully dry out. The wood stays damp 8-10 months of the year. Add any overflow from gutters — even small leaks at seams — and you've got wood that's saturated from both sides.</p>

<p>Saturated wood rots. Not immediately, but inevitably. The timeline depends on the wood species, the paint quality, and how much direct water exposure there is. But we see fascia rot on lake properties that's 3-4x more severe than the same age house in Huntsville.</p>

<p>The vacation home problem makes it worse. A full-time resident notices when paint starts peeling or wood looks discolored. A vacation home might go six months between visits. What started as a soft spot in March is 12 inches of rot by September. We've seen lake houses where the owner had no idea until gutters started pulling away from the house.</p>

<p>The older homes in <a href="/recent-projects/">downtown Scottsboro</a> face a different version of this. Original fascia from the 60s and 70s, painted over multiple times, with 50+ years of accumulated weather exposure. That wood is tired. Many of those homes need fascia work regardless of current gutter condition.</p>

<p>The critical issue: installing new gutters on rotted fascia is throwing money away. The hangers won't hold. The gutters will sag, pull away, or fall off entirely within a few years. Any legitimate gutter contractor checks fascia condition before quoting. If they don't mention it, they're either not looking or not telling you.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>We start with inspection. Not from the ground — we get up there and actually check the wood. Push on it. Look for soft spots. Check behind existing gutters where moisture collects. For vacation homes, we photograph everything and send you the documentation.</p>

<p>If we find damage, we measure the extent. Rot spreads — a visible 6-inch bad spot usually means 12-18 inches of compromised wood that looks okay from outside but fails the push test. We quote for the full extent of damage, not just what's visible.</p>

<p>The repair process: remove existing gutters, cut out all damaged fascia (and soffit if affected), inspect underlying structure for rot spread to rafter tails. Install new fascia board — we use primed finger-joint pine or composite board depending on exposure and preference. Prime and paint to match existing. Then install gutters on solid wood.</p>

<p>For <a href="/recent-projects/">lake properties</a>, we typically recommend composite or PVC fascia board. Costs about 30% more than primed pine but handles humidity dramatically better. Won't rot, won't absorb moisture, outlasts wood by decades. If you're already paying for labor to replace fascia once, the material upgrade often makes sense.</p>

<p>What we don't do: caulk over soft spots and hope for the best. Install gutters on questionable fascia and wish you luck. Hide the damage and let the next guy deal with it. If the wood's bad, we tell you. What you do with that information is up to you, but we won't pretend the problem doesn't exist.</p>
{% endset %}

